+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xw67GSBqurnc7gwqdi1kmN","type":"tool_result","content":"=== phases/phase-01.md ===\n# Phase 1: Basic FIFO Queue\n\nImplement a `TaskQueue` class with the following interface:\n\n## Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(payload)`** - Add a task to the queue. Returns the created task object.\n- **`dequeue()`** - Remove and return the next task in FIFO order. Returns `null` if the queue is empty. Sets the task status to `processing`.\n- **`size()`** - Return the number of pending tasks in the queue.\n\n## Task Object\n\nEach task has the following properties:\n\n| Property    | Type     | Description                                    |\n|-------------|----------|------------------------------------------------|\n| `id`        | `string` | Auto-generated unique identifier (UUID format) |\n| `payload`   | `any`    | The data associated with the task              |\n| `status`    | `string` | One of: `pending`, `processing`, `completed`, `failed` |\n| `createdAt` | `Date`   | Timestamp when the task was created            |\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Tasks are returned in the order they were added (First-In, First-Out)\n- Each task gets a unique `id` automatically assigned\n- New tasks start with `status: 'pending'`\n- When dequeued, task status changes to `'processing'`\n- `dequeue()` returns `null` when the queue is empty\n\n## Export\n\nExport the `TaskQueue` class as a named export from `src/index.ts`.\n\n=== phases/phase-02.md ===\n# Phase 2: Named Queues\n\nExtend the `TaskQueue` to support multiple named queues.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload)`** - Add a task to a specific named queue. Returns the created task.\n- **`dequeue(queueName)`** - Get the next task from a specific named queue. Returns `null` if queue is empty or doesn't exist.\n- **`listQueues()`** - Return an array of all queue names that have been created.\n- **`queueSize(queueName)`** - Return the number of pending tasks in a specific queue.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Each named queue operates independently\n- Enqueueing to a queue that doesn't exist creates it\n- Dequeueing from a non-existent queue returns `null`\n- If no queue name is specified, use `'default'` as the queue name\n- `listQueues()` returns names of all queues that have been created\n- Queue-specific sizes are tracked independently\n\n## Backward Compatibility\n\nThe single-argument `enqueue(payload)` should still work by using the `'default'` queue.\nSimilarly, `dequeue()` with no arguments should dequeue from `'default'`.\n\n=== phases/phase-03.md ===\n# Phase 3: Priority\n\nAdd priority support to tasks.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload, options?)`** - The options object can include `priority`.\n  - `priority`: number from 1 (highest) to 10 (lowest). Default is 5.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- `dequeue()` returns the highest priority task first (lowest number = highest priority)\n- Within the same priority level, FIFO order is preserved\n- Default priority is 5 if not specified\n- Priority values outside 1-10 should throw an error\n- Priority is per-queue (each named queue has its own priority ordering)\n- The task object should include a `priority` property\n\n=== phases/phase-04.md ===\n# Phase 4: Delayed/Scheduled Tasks\n\nAdd support for delayed and scheduled task execution.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload, options?)`** - Options can now include:\n  - `delay`: number of milliseconds before the task becomes available\n  - `runAt`: a `Date` object specifying when the task should become available\n  - (existing) `priority`: priority level\n\n## Requirements\n\n- A delayed task is enqueued immediately but not returned by `dequeue()` until the delay has elapsed\n- A `runAt` task becomes available at the specified time\n- Non-delayed tasks should be dequeued before delayed tasks that haven't become available yet\n- When multiple delayed tasks are available, they are ordered by their availability time (earliest first), then by priority, then FIFO\n- `delay` and `runAt` work with named queues\n- The task object should include a `availableAt` property (Date) indicating when the task becomes available\n\n=== phases/phase-05.md ===\n# Phase 5: Retry with Backoff\n\nAdd automatic retry functionality with exponential backoff.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`fail(taskId, error?)`** - Mark a processing task as failed. Triggers automatic retry if retries remain.\n- **`complete(taskId)`** - Mark a processing task as completed.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload, options?)`** - Options can now include:\n  - `maxRetries`: maximum number of retry attempts (default: 3)\n  - `backoffMs`: base delay for exponential backoff in milliseconds (default: 1000)\n\n## Requirements\n\n- When `fail()` is called and retries remain, the task is re-enqueued with a delay\n- Backoff formula: `backoffMs * 2^(attempt - 1)` where attempt starts at 1\n  - 1st retry: 1000ms, 2nd retry: 2000ms, 3rd retry: 4000ms\n- `task.attempts` tracks how many times the task has been attempted (starts at 1 on first dequeue)\n- Custom `maxRetries` can be set per task\n- Retried tasks preserve their original queue name and priority\n- If a task has exhausted all retries, it remains in `failed` status (see Phase 6 for DLQ)\n\n=== phases/phase-06.md ===\n# Phase 6: Dead Letter Queue\n\nAdd dead letter queue (DLQ) support for tasks that have exhausted all retries.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`getDLQ(queueName)`** - Return an array of all dead-letter tasks for the specified queue.\n- **`retryDLQ(taskId)`** - Move a dead-letter task back to its original queue for another round of retries. Returns the re-enqueued task.\n- **`clearDLQ(queueName)`** - Remove all dead-letter tasks for the specified queue.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- When a task exhausts all retry attempts (from Phase 5), it automatically moves to the DLQ\n- DLQ tasks have status `'failed'` and are stored separately from the main queue\n- Each DLQ task retains its original error information (`task.lastError`)\n- DLQ tasks track how many times they've failed (`task.failCount`)\n- `retryDLQ(taskId)` resets the task's retry count, giving it a fresh set of retries\n- `retryDLQ(taskId)` places the task back in its original queue with original priority\n- `clearDLQ(queueName)` removes all DLQ entries for that queue\n- DLQ is per-queue (each named queue has its own DLQ)\n\n=== phases/phase-07.md ===\n# Phase 7: Task Dependencies\n\nAdd support for task dependencies, where tasks can wait for other tasks to complete before becoming available.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload, options?)`** - Options can now include:\n  - `dependsOn`: an array of task IDs that must complete before this task becomes available\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`getDependencies(taskId)`** - Return dependency information for a task, including each dependency's ID and current status.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- A task with dependencies remains blocked (not available for dequeue) until ALL dependencies have `completed` status\n- When a dependency completes (via `complete(taskId)`), the system checks if any dependent tasks are now unblocked\n- Circular dependencies must be detected and throw an error at enqueue time\n  - Direct circular: A depends on B, B depends on A\n  - Indirect circular: A → B → C → A\n- `getDependencies(taskId)` returns an array of `{ taskId, status }` objects\n- Dependency on a non-existent task ID should throw an error\n- A failed dependency does NOT auto-fail the dependent task - it stays blocked\n- Cascading dependencies work: if A depends on B and B depends on C, A is only available after both B and C complete\n\n=== phases/phase-08.md ===\n# Phase 8: Concurrency Control\n\nAdd per-queue concurrency limits to control how many tasks can be processed simultaneously.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`setConcurrency(queueName, limit)`** - Set the maximum number of tasks that can be in `processing` status simultaneously for a queue.\n- **`getConcurrency(queueName)`** - Return concurrency information: `{ current: number, max: number }`.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- `dequeue()` returns `null` if the queue has reached its concurrency limit, even if tasks are available\n- `complete(taskId)` and `fail(taskId)` free up a concurrency slot\n- Default concurrency is unlimited (no limit) until `setConcurrency` is called\n- Each queue has its own independent concurrency limit\n- Concurrency interacts correctly with priorities (highest priority task still chosen, but only if a slot is available)\n- `getConcurrency(queueName)` returns the current number of processing tasks and the configured maximum\n  - For unlimited queues, `max` should be `Infinity` or `0` to indicate no limit\n\n=== phases/phase-09.md ===\n# Phase 9: Progress and Cancellation\n\nAdd progress tracking and task cancellation support.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`updateProgress(taskId, percent, message?)`** - Report progress for a processing task (0-100).\n- **`getProgress(taskId)`** - Return progress info: `{ percent: number, message?: string, updatedAt: Date }`.\n- **`cancel(taskId)`** - Cancel a pending or processing task. Sets status to `'cancelled'`.\n- **`onProgress(taskId, callback)`** - Subscribe to progress updates. Callback receives `{ percent, message, updatedAt }`.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Progress percentage must be between 0 and 100\n- `updateProgress` fires all registered `onProgress` callbacks for that task\n- `cancel()` works on both `pending` and `processing` tasks\n- A cancelled pending task is removed from the queue (not available for dequeue)\n- A cancelled processing task has its status set to `'cancelled'`\n- Cancelled tasks are NOT retried (even if they have retries remaining)\n- `getProgress(taskId)` returns `null` if no progress has been reported yet\n- The `message` parameter in `updateProgress` is optional\n- `onProgress` returns an unsubscribe function\n\n=== phases/phase-10.md ===\n# Phase 10: Recurring Tasks (Cron)\n\nAdd support for recurring scheduled tasks using simple cron-like expressions.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`schedule(queueName, cronExpression, taskTemplate)`** - Create a recurring task schedule. Returns a schedule object with an `id`.\n- **`unschedule(scheduleId)`** - Remove a schedule, stopping future occurrences.\n- **`listSchedules(queueName?)`** - List all active schedules, optionally filtered by queue name.\n- **`tick()`** - Manually advance and check schedules, triggering any that are due. For testing purposes.\n\n## Cron Expression Subset\n\nSupport a simple subset of cron expressions:\n\n| Expression       | Meaning              |\n|------------------|----------------------|\n| `*/N * * * *`    | Every N minutes      |\n| `0 * * * *`      | Every hour (on the hour) |\n| `0 0 * * *`      | Every day at midnight |\n\n## Requirements\n\n- `schedule()` returns a schedule object: `{ id, queueName, cronExpression, taskTemplate, active: true }`\n- `tick()` checks all active schedules and enqueues tasks for any that are due\n- The `taskTemplate` is used as the payload for enqueued tasks\n- `unschedule(scheduleId)` marks the schedule as inactive\n- `listSchedules()` returns only active schedules\n- Multiple schedules can coexist on the same or different queues\n- Scheduled tasks are regular tasks once enqueued (subject to all other queue rules)\n\n=== phases/phase-11.md ===\n# Phase 11: Middleware Pipeline\n\nAdd a middleware system for queue processing pipelines.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`use(queueName, middleware)`** - Add a middleware function to a queue's processing pipeline.\n- **`process(queueName, handler)`** - Register a task handler for a queue and begin processing. The handler runs after all middleware.\n\n## Middleware Signature\n\n```typescript\ntype Middleware = (task: Task, next: () => Promise<void>) => Promise<void>;\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Middleware functions run in the order they were added (first added = first to run)\n- Each middleware must call `next()` to pass control to the next middleware (and eventually the handler)\n- If a middleware does NOT call `next()`, the task processing is skipped\n- Middleware can modify the task object (e.g., add metadata)\n- If a middleware throws an error, the task is marked as failed\n- Each queue has its own independent middleware stack\n- Middleware receives the full task context (task object)\n- `process()` dequeues a task and runs it through the middleware pipeline, then the handler\n\n=== phases/phase-12.md ===\n# Phase 12: Graceful Shutdown\n\nAdd graceful shutdown capabilities to the task queue.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`shutdown(timeout?)`** - Initiate graceful shutdown. Returns a Promise that resolves with a summary.\n  - `timeout`: maximum milliseconds to wait for processing tasks (default: 30000)\n- **`onShutdown(callback)`** - Register a callback to be called during shutdown.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- After `shutdown()` is called, `enqueue()` should throw an error\n- `shutdown()` waits for all currently processing tasks to complete\n- If processing tasks don't complete within the timeout, they are force-cancelled\n- `onShutdown` callbacks are called during the shutdown process\n- The returned summary object includes:\n  - `completed`: number of tasks that completed during shutdown\n  - `cancelled`: number of tasks that were force-cancelled\n  - `elapsed`: time in milliseconds the shutdown took\n- Multiple calls to `shutdown()` should be idempotent (return the same promise/result)\n- If there are no processing tasks, shutdown resolves immediately\n- Force-cancelled tasks have their status set to `'cancelled'`","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0c09c517-6e09-4b3b-a588-57cd9af7e60d","uuid":"b10be66a-e7d5-4b4e-b0b9-463247872a64","timestamp":"2026-07-01T05:10:32.775Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== phases/phase-01.md ===\n# Phase 1: Basic FIFO Queue\n\nImplement a `TaskQueue` class with the following interface:\n\n## Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(payload)`** - Add a task to the queue. Returns the created task object.\n- **`dequeue()`** - Remove and return the next task in FIFO order. Returns `null` if the queue is empty. Sets the task status to `processing`.\n- **`size()`** - Return the number of pending tasks in the queue.\n\n## Task Object\n\nEach task has the following properties:\n\n| Property    | Type     | Description                                    |\n|-------------|----------|------------------------------------------------|\n| `id`        | `string` | Auto-generated unique identifier (UUID format) |\n| `payload`   | `any`    | The data associated with the task              |\n| `status`    | `string` | One of: `pending`, `processing`, `completed`, `failed` |\n| `createdAt` | `Date`   | Timestamp when the task was created            |\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Tasks are returned in the order they were added (First-In, First-Out)\n- Each task gets a unique `id` automatically assigned\n- New tasks start with `status: 'pending'`\n- When dequeued, task status changes to `'processing'`\n- `dequeue()` returns `null` when the queue is empty\n\n## Export\n\nExport the `TaskQueue` class as a named export from `src/index.ts`.\n\n=== phases/phase-02.md ===\n# Phase 2: Named Queues\n\nExtend the `TaskQueue` to support multiple named queues.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload)`** - Add a task to a specific named queue. Returns the created task.\n- **`dequeue(queueName)`** - Get the next task from a specific named queue. Returns `null` if queue is empty or doesn't exist.\n- **`listQueues()`** - Return an array of all queue names that have been created.\n- **`queueSize(queueName)`** - Return the number of pending tasks in a specific queue.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Each named queue operates independently\n- Enqueueing to a queue that doesn't exist creates it\n- Dequeueing from a non-existent queue returns `null`\n- If no queue name is specified, use `'default'` as the queue name\n- `listQueues()` returns names of all queues that have been created\n- Queue-specific sizes are tracked independently\n\n## Backward Compatibility\n\nThe single-argument `enqueue(payload)` should still work by using the `'default'` queue.\nSimilarly, `dequeue()` with no arguments should dequeue from `'default'`.\n\n=== phases/phase-03.md ===\n# Phase 3: Priority\n\nAdd priority support to tasks.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload, options?)`** - The options object can include `priority`.\n  - `priority`: number from 1 (highest) to 10 (lowest). Default is 5.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- `dequeue()` returns the highest priority task first (lowest number = highest priority)\n- Within the same priority level, FIFO order is preserved\n- Default priority is 5 if not specified\n- Priority values outside 1-10 should throw an error\n- Priority is per-queue (each named queue has its own priority ordering)\n- The task object should include a `priority` property\n\n=== phases/phase-04.md ===\n# Phase 4: Delayed/Scheduled Tasks\n\nAdd support for delayed and scheduled task execution.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload, options?)`** - Options can now include:\n  - `delay`: number of milliseconds before the task becomes available\n  - `runAt`: a `Date` object specifying when the task should become available\n  - (existing) `priority`: priority level\n\n## Requirements\n\n- A delayed task is enqueued immediately but not returned by `dequeue()` until the delay has elapsed\n- A `runAt` task becomes available at the specified time\n- Non-delayed tasks should be dequeued before delayed tasks that haven't become available yet\n- When multiple delayed tasks are available, they are ordered by their availability time (earliest first), then by priority, then FIFO\n- `delay` and `runAt` work with named queues\n- The task object should include a `availableAt` property (Date) indicating when the task becomes available\n\n=== phases/phase-05.md ===\n# Phase 5: Retry with Backoff\n\nAdd automatic retry functionality with exponential backoff.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`fail(taskId, error?)`** - Mark a processing task as failed. Triggers automatic retry if retries remain.\n- **`complete(taskId)`** - Mark a processing task as completed.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload, options?)`** - Options can now include:\n  - `maxRetries`: maximum number of retry attempts (default: 3)\n  - `backoffMs`: base delay for exponential backoff in milliseconds (default: 1000)\n\n## Requirements\n\n- When `fail()` is called and retries remain, the task is re-enqueued with a delay\n- Backoff formula: `backoffMs * 2^(attempt - 1)` where attempt starts at 1\n  - 1st retry: 1000ms, 2nd retry: 2000ms, 3rd retry: 4000ms\n- `task.attempts` tracks how many times the task has been attempted (starts at 1 on first dequeue)\n- Custom `maxRetries` can be set per task\n- Retried tasks preserve their original queue name and priority\n- If a task has exhausted all retries, it remains in `failed` status (see Phase 6 for DLQ)\n\n=== phases/phase-06.md ===\n# Phase 6: Dead Letter Queue\n\nAdd dead letter queue (DLQ) support for tasks that have exhausted all retries.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`getDLQ(queueName)`** - Return an array of all dead-letter tasks for the specified queue.\n- **`retryDLQ(taskId)`** - Move a dead-letter task back to its original queue for another round of retries. Returns the re-enqueued task.\n- **`clearDLQ(queueName)`** - Remove all dead-letter tasks for the specified queue.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- When a task exhausts all retry attempts (from Phase 5), it automatically moves to the DLQ\n- DLQ tasks have status `'failed'` and are stored separately from the main queue\n- Each DLQ task retains its original error information (`task.lastError`)\n- DLQ tasks track how many times they've failed (`task.failCount`)\n- `retryDLQ(taskId)` resets the task's retry count, giving it a fresh set of retries\n- `retryDLQ(taskId)` places the task back in its original queue with original priority\n- `clearDLQ(queueName)` removes all DLQ entries for that queue\n- DLQ is per-queue (each named queue has its own DLQ)\n\n=== phases/phase-07.md ===\n# Phase 7: Task Dependencies\n\nAdd support for task dependencies, where tasks can wait for other tasks to complete before becoming available.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload, options?)`** - Options can now include:\n  - `dependsOn`: an array of task IDs that must complete before this task becomes available\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`getDependencies(taskId)`** - Return dependency information for a task, including each dependency's ID and current status.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- A task with dependencies remains blocked (not available for dequeue) until ALL dependencies have `completed` status\n- When a dependency completes (via `complete(taskId)`), the system checks if any dependent tasks are now unblocked\n- Circular dependencies must be detected and throw an error at enqueue time\n  - Direct circular: A depends on B, B depends on A\n  - Indirect circular: A → B → C → A\n- `getDependencies(taskId)` returns an array of `{ taskId, status }` objects\n- Dependency on a non-existent task ID should throw an error\n- A failed dependency does NOT auto-fail the dependent task - it stays blocked\n- Cascading dependencies work: if A depends on B and B depends on C, A is only available after both B and C complete\n\n=== phases/phase-08.md ===\n# Phase 8: Concurrency Control\n\nAdd per-queue concurrency limits to control how many tasks can be processed simultaneously.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`setConcurrency(queueName, limit)`** - Set the maximum number of tasks that can be in `processing` status simultaneously for a queue.\n- **`getConcurrency(queueName)`** - Return concurrency information: `{ current: number, max: number }`.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- `dequeue()` returns `null` if the queue has reached its concurrency limit, even if tasks are available\n- `complete(taskId)` and `fail(taskId)` free up a concurrency slot\n- Default concurrency is unlimited (no limit) until `setConcurrency` is called\n- Each queue has its own independent concurrency limit\n- Concurrency interacts correctly with priorities (highest priority task still chosen, but only if a slot is available)\n- `getConcurrency(queueName)` returns the current number of processing tasks and the configured maximum\n  - For unlimited queues, `max` should be `Infinity` or `0` to indicate no limit\n\n=== phases/phase-09.md ===\n# Phase 9: Progress and Cancellation\n\nAdd progress tracking and task cancellation support.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`updateProgress(taskId, percent, message?)`** - Report progress for a processing task (0-100).\n- **`getProgress(taskId)`** - Return progress info: `{ percent: number, message?: string, updatedAt: Date }`.\n- **`cancel(taskId)`** - Cancel a pending or processing task. Sets status to `'cancelled'`.\n- **`onProgress(taskId, callback)`** - Subscribe to progress updates. Callback receives `{ percent, message, updatedAt }`.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Progress percentage must be between 0 and 100\n- `updateProgress` fires all registered `onProgress` callbacks for that task\n- `cancel()` works on both `pending` and `processing` tasks\n- A cancelled pending task is removed from the queue (not available for dequeue)\n- A cancelled processing task has its status set to `'cancelled'`\n- Cancelled tasks are NOT retried (even if they have retries remaining)\n- `getProgress(taskId)` returns `null` if no progress has been reported yet\n- The `message` parameter in `updateProgress` is optional\n- `onProgress` returns an unsubscribe function\n\n=== phases/phase-10.md ===\n# Phase 10: Recurring Tasks (Cron)\n\nAdd support for recurring scheduled tasks using simple cron-like expressions.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`schedule(queueName, cronExpression, taskTemplate)`** - Create a recurring task schedule. Returns a schedule object with an `id`.\n- **`unschedule(scheduleId)`** - Remove a schedule, stopping future occurrences.\n- **`listSchedules(queueName?)`** - List all active schedules, optionally filtered by queue name.\n- **`tick()`** - Manually advance and check schedules, triggering any that are due. For testing purposes.\n\n## Cron Expression Subset\n\nSupport a simple subset of cron expressions:\n\n| Expression       | Meaning              |\n|------------------|----------------------|\n| `*/N * * * *`    | Every N minutes      |\n| `0 * * * *`      | Every hour (on the hour) |\n| `0 0 * * *`      | Every day at midnight |\n\n## Requirements\n\n- `schedule()` returns a schedule object: `{ id, queueName, cronExpression, taskTemplate, active: true }`\n- `tick()` checks all active schedules and enqueues tasks for any that are due\n- The `taskTemplate` is used as the payload for enqueued tasks\n- `unschedule(scheduleId)` marks the schedule as inactive\n- `listSchedules()` returns only active schedules\n- Multiple schedules can coexist on the same or different queues\n- Scheduled tasks are regular tasks once enqueued (subject to all other queue rules)\n\n=== phases/phase-11.md ===\n# Phase 11: Middleware Pipeline\n\nAdd a middleware system for queue processing pipelines.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`use(queueName, middleware)`** - Add a middleware function to a queue's processing pipeline.\n- **`process(queueName, handler)`** - Register a task handler for a queue and begin processing. The handler runs after all middleware.\n\n## Middleware Signature\n\n```typescript\ntype Middleware = (task: Task, next: () => Promise<void>) => Promise<void>;\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Middleware functions run in the order they were added (first added = first to run)\n- Each middleware must call `next()` to pass control to the next middleware (and eventually the handler)\n- If a middleware does NOT call `next()`, the task processing is skipped\n- Middleware can modify the task object (e.g., add metadata)\n- If a middleware throws an error, the task is marked as failed\n- Each queue has its own independent middleware stack\n- Middleware receives the full task context (task object)\n- `process()` dequeues a task and runs it through the middleware pipeline, then the handler\n\n=== phases/phase-12.md ===\n# Phase 12: Graceful Shutdown\n\nAdd graceful shutdown capabilities to the task queue.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`shutdown(timeout?)`** - Initiate graceful shutdown. Returns a Promise that resolves with a summary.\n  - `timeout`: maximum milliseconds to wait for processing tasks (default: 30000)\n- **`onShutdown(callback)`** - Register a callback to be called during shutdown.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- After `shutdown()` is called, `enqueue()` should throw an error\n- `shutdown()` waits for all currently processing tasks to complete\n- If processing tasks don't complete within the timeout, they are force-cancelled\n- `onShutdown` callbacks are called during the shutdown process\n- The returned summary object includes:\n  - `completed`: number of tasks that completed during shutdown\n  - `cancelled`: number of tasks that were force-cancelled\n  - `elapsed`: time in milliseconds the shutdown took\n- Multiple calls to `shutdown()` should be idempotent (return the same promise/result)\n- If there are no processing tasks, shutdown resolves immediately\n- Force-cancelled tasks have their status set to `'cancelled'`","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
